Since \( f(n + 1) = 1 \),
\[ 1 \mid 1 + n \implies n \text{ is any positive integer}. \]

For the third solution, assume \( f(1) = n \), \( f(x) = 1 \) for odd \( x > 1 \), and \( f(x) = 2 \) for even \( x > 1 \). Then for \( x = 1 \) and \( y = 1 \),
\[ f(n + 1) \mid 1 + n. \]
Since \( f(n + 1) = 1 \) if \( n + 1 \) is odd and \( f(n + 1) = 2 \) if \( n + 1 \) is even,
\[ 1 \mid 1 + n \text{ and } 2 \mid 1 + n \implies n \text{ is odd}. \]

Thus, the solutions are:
1. \( f(x) = x \),
2. \( f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \end{cases} \) for any \( n \),
3. \( f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \) for any \( n \) odd.
  [7] #### Proof of Lemma
Assume \( f \) is not injective. Then, there exist \( a, b \in \mathbb{Z}_+ \) with \( a \neq b \) such that \( f(a) = f(b) \). Let \( c = f(a) = f(b) \). For any \( y \in \mathbb{Z}_+ \), we have \( f(c + y) \mid a + f(y) \) and \( f(c + y) \mid b + f(y) \). Thus, \( f(c + y) \mid (a - b) \). Since \( a \neq b \), \( a - b \) is a fixed non-zero integer. Therefore, \( f(c + y) \) is bounded, proving the lemma.

#### Case 1: \( f \) is injective
If \( f \) is injective, then \( f(f(x) + y) \mid x + f(y) \) implies \( f(f(x) + y) = 1 \) for some \( y \). Let \( y = 1 \), then \( f(f(x) + 1) = 1 \). Since \( f \) is injective, \( f(x) + 1 \) must be a constant, say \( k \). Thus, \( f(x) = k - 1 \) for all \( x \). Substituting \( f(x) = k - 1 \) into the original equation, we get \( f(k - 1 + y) = 1 \mid x + (k - 1) \), which implies \( k - 1 + y \) must be 1, a contradiction unless \( k = 1 \). Hence, \( f(x) = x \).

#### Case 2: \( f \) is bounded
If \( f \) is bounded, let \( M \) be the maximum value of \( f \). Then, for any \( x \), \( f(x) \leq M \). Let \( y = M \), then \( f(f(x) + M) \mid x + f(M) \). Since \( f(f(x) + M) \leq M \), \( M \mid x + f(M) \). This implies \( f(M) = M \). Now, let \( x = 1 \), then \( f(f(1) + y) \mid 1 + f(y) \). Since \( f(f(1) + y) \leq M \), \( M \mid 1 + f(y) \). Thus, \( f(y) \) is constant for \( y > 1 \). Let \( f(1) = n \), then \( f(n + y) = 1 \) for \( y > 1 \). If \( n > 1 \), then \( f(n + 1) = 1 \), but \( f(n + 1) \leq M \), a contradiction unless \( n = 1 \). Hence, \( f(1) = 1 \) and \( f(x) = 1 \) for \( x > 1 \).

#### Case 3: Mixed behavior
If \( f \) is not injective and not bounded, consider \( f(1) = n \) and \( f(x) = 1 \) for \( x > 1 \) odd, and \( f(x) = 2 \) for \( x > 1 \) even. This satisfies the original equation.

Thus, the solutions are:
1. \( f(x) = x \),
2. \( f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \end{cases} \) for any \( n \),
3. \( f(x) = \begin{cases} n & \text{if } x = 1 \\ 1 & \text{if } x > 1 \text{ is odd} \\ 2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \end{cases} \) for any \( n \) odd.
