Thus, \( f(t + y) \mid (a - b) \). Since \( y \) can be arbitrarily large, \( f(t + y) \) can take arbitrarily large values, implying \( a = b \) or \( f \) is bounded. If \( f \) is bounded, then \( f \) is constant, which contradicts the divisibility condition unless the constant is 1. Therefore, \( f \) is injective.

Next, let \( f(1) = c \). For \( x = 1 \),
\[ f(c + y) \mid 1 + f(y). \]
Since \( f \) is injective, \( f(c + y) \) must take distinct values for distinct \( y \). If \( c > 1 \), then for large enough \( y \), \( f(c + y) \) will exceed \( 1 + f(y) \), a contradiction. Thus, \( c = 1 \), so \( f(1) = 1 \).

Now, let \( y = 1 \),
\[ f(f(x) + 1) \mid x + 1. \]
Since \( f \) is injective and \( f(1) = 1 \), \( f(x) \) must be \( x \) for all \( x \). Therefore, \( f(x) = x \) is the only solution.
