If \( f \) is bounded and injective, \( f \) must be the identity function. Suppose \( f(1) = n \). Then \( f(n + y) \mid 1 + f(y) \). If \( n > 1 \), \( f(n + y) \) can only be 1, contradicting \( f \) being injective. Thus, \( f(1) = 1 \).

