$$
f(f(1) + y) \mid 1 + f(y).
$$
Since \( f \) is injective, \( f(1) + y \neq 1 + f(y) \) for \( y > 0 \). Therefore, \( f(f(1) + y) \leq 1 + f(y) \). For \( y = 1 \), we have
$$
f(f(1) + 1) \mid 2 + f(1).
$$
If \( f(1) > 1 \), then \( f(f(1) + 1) \leq 2 + f(1) \) and \( f(f(1) + 1) > 2 \), which is a contradiction. Thus, \( f(1) = 1 \).

Now, we prove that \( f(x) = x \) for all \( x \in \mathbb{Z}_+ \). Setting \( y = 1 \) in the original equation, we get
$$
f(f(x) + 1) \mid x + f(1) = x + 1.
$$
Since \( f \) is injective, \( f(x) + 1 \leq x + 1 \), implying \( f(x) \leq x \). Setting \( x = f(x) \) in the original equation, we get
$$
f(f(f(x)) + y) \mid f(x) + f(y).
$$
Since \( f(f(x)) \leq f(x) \), we have \( f(f(f(x)) + y) \leq f(x) + y \). For \( y = 1 \), we get
$$
f(f(f(x)) + 1) \mid f(x) + 1.
$$
Since \( f(f(x)) \leq f(x) \), \( f(f(f(x)) + 1) \leq f(x) + 1 \), and \( f(f(f(x)) + 1) \geq f(x) + 1 \), it follows that \( f(f(f(x)) + 1) = f(x) + 1 \). By injectivity, \( f(f(x)) = f(x) \), and thus \( f(x) = x \).

Therefore, the only solution is \( f(x) = x \).
