This simplifies to \( f(1,4) f(4,c) + f(2,4) f(4,c) + f(3,4) f(4,c) = 3(n-2) \) for all \( c \in \{5, 6, \ldots, n\} \). Since \( f(2,4) = k \) and \( f(3,4) = k \), we have \( f(1,4) k + k f(4,c) + k f(4,c) = 3(n-2) \). For \( c \in \{5, 6, \ldots, n\} \), \( f(4,c) = k \), so \( f(1,4) k + 2k^2 = 3(n-2) \). This implies \( k(f(1,4) + 2k) = 3(n-2) \).

By comparing the equations \( k(f(1,3) + k) = 2(n-2) \) and \( k(f(1,4) + 2k) = 3(n-2) \), we see that \( f(1,3) + k = 2k \) and \( f(1,4) + 2k = 3k \). This gives \( f(1,3) = k \) and \( f(1,4) = k \). By similar arguments, we find \( f(a,b) = k \) for all \( a, b \in \{1, 2, \ldots, n\} \).

Thus, the function \( W \) must be a constant function. Let \( W(a,b) = c \) for all \( a, b \in \{1, 2, \ldots, n\} \). Then the given condition becomes:
\[
