\[
f(2^{x(a+1)-y} p) \leq \sigma(2^{x(a+1)-y} p) - 2^{x(a+1)-y+1} p < 2^{x(a+1)-y} p - 2^{x(a+1)-y+1} p < 0,
\]
so \( 2^{x(a+1)-y} p \notin S \).

Assume \( 2^r p \notin S \) for some \( r = x(a+1) - y \) with \( 0 \leq y \leq a \). Then for \( r' = (x+1)(a+1) - y \),
\[
2^{r'} p = 2^{(x+1)(a+1)-y} p = 2^{x(a+1)+a+1-y} p = 2^{x(a+1)-y} p \cdot 2^{a+1} \in S,
\]
which contradicts the assumption. Therefore, \( 2^r p \in S \) if and only if \( a+1 \nmid r \).

Thus, the positive integers \( n = 2^k \cdot p \) such that \( \sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d = n \) are those for which \( a+1 \nmid k \), where \( a = \lfloor \log_2(p+1) \rfloor - 1 \).
  [18] For the base case \( x = 1 \), we have \( r = a - y \). If \( y = 0 \), then \( r = a \), and \( 2^a p \notin S \) because \( 2^a p \) has no divisors in \( S \) that sum to \( 2^a p \). If \( 1 \leq y \leq a \), then \( 2^{a-y} p \in S \) and \( 2^{a-y+1} p \notin S \). Thus, the base case holds.

For the inductive step, assume \( 2^{x(a+1) - y} p \notin S \) for some \( x \geq 1 \) and \( 0 \leq y \leq a \). Then \( 2^{(x+1)(a+1) - y} p \notin S \) because the divisors of \( 2^{(x+1)(a+1) - y} p \) that are in \( S \) are of the form \( 2^t \cdot d \) where \( t \leq (x+1)(a+1) - y \) and \( d \) is a divisor of \( p \) in \( S \), but the sum of these divisors is less than \( 2^{(x+1)(a+1) - y} p \).

Therefore, \( 2^r p \notin S \) if and only if \( a+1 \mid r \).

Finally, for \( n = 2^k p \) where \( a+1 \nmid k \), we have \( \sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d < n \). For \( n = 2^k p \) where \( a+1 \mid k \), we have \( \sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d = n \).

Thus, the positive integers \( n = 2^k p \) such that \( \sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d = n \) are exactly those where \( k \) is a multiple of \( \lfloor \log_2(p+1) \rfloor \).
  [19] For \( x = 1 \) and \( y > 0 \), we have \( r \leq a \). Then,
\[
f(2^{x(a+1)-y} p) = f(2^{a+1} \cdot 2^{x(a+1)-y-a-1} p) = f(2^{a+1} \cdot 2^{x-1} p) = f(2^{a+1} p) + f(2^{x-1} p) + 2^{a+1} \cdot 2^{x-1} p - 2^{a+1} p \leq 0.
\]
Since \( 2^{a+1} p \notin S \), we have \( f(2^{a+1} p) \leq 0 \). By the induction hypothesis, \( 2^{x-1} p \notin S \), so \( f(2^{x-1} p) \leq 0 \). Thus, \( f(2^{x(a+1)-y} p) \leq 0 \), proving the base case.

For the inductive step, assume \( 2^{x(a+1)-y} p \notin S \) for some \( x \geq 1 \) and \( 0 \leq y \leq a \). Then,
\[
f(2^{(x+1)(a+1)-y} p) = f(2^{a+1} \cdot 2^{x(a+1)-y} p) = f(2^{a+1} \cdot 2^{x(a+1)-y-a-1} p) = f(2^{a+1} \cdot 2^{x-1} p) + f(2^{x(a+1)-y} p) + 2^{a+1} \cdot 2^{x-1} p - 2^{a+1} p \leq 0.
\]
Since \( 2^{x(a+1)-y} p \notin S \), we have \( f(2^{x(a+1)-y} p) \leq 0 \). By the induction hypothesis, \( 2^{x-1} p \notin S \), so \( f(2^{x-1} p) \leq 0 \). Thus, \( f(2^{(x+1)(a+1)-y} p) \leq 0 \), completing the inductive step.

Therefore, \( 2^r p \notin S \) if and only if \( a+1 \mid r \). For \( 2^r p \in S \), we need \( f(2^r p) = 0 \). This implies \( r = a \). Thus, the only \( n = 2^k \cdot p \) such that \( \sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d = n \) is \( n = 2^a p \) where \( a = \lfloor \log_2(p+1) \rfloor - 1 \).
