**Inductive Step:**
Assume \( 2^{x(a+1)-y} p \in S \) for \( 0 \leq y \leq a \) and \( x \geq 1 \). Then,
\[
f(2^{(x+1)(a+1)-y} p) \leq \sigma(2^{(x+1)(a+1)-y} p) - 2^{(x+1)(a+1)-y+1} p \leq 0,
\]
so \( 2^{(x+1)(a+1)-y} p \in S \) for \( 0 \leq y \leq a \).

For \( y = a+1 \),
\[
f(2^{(x+1)(a+1)} p) = 2^{(x+1)(a+2)} - 2^{(x+1)(a+1)+1} p > 0,
\]
so \( 2^{(x+1)(a+1)} p \notin S \).

Thus, \( 2^r p \notin S \) if and only if \( a+1 \mid r \). Therefore, \( 2^r p \in S \) if and only if \( a+1 \nmid r \).

The final result is that \( n = 2^k p \) is in \( S \) if and only if \( k \) is not a multiple of \( \lfloor \log_2(p+1) \rfloor \).
  [23] For \( x = 1 \) and \( y = 0 \), we have \( f(2^{a+1} p) = 2^{a+2} - (p + 1) > 0 \), so \( 2^{a+1} p \notin S \).

Assume the claim holds for \( x \). For \( x+1 \) and \( y > 0 \),
$$
f(2^{x(a+1) + r} p) \leq \sigma(2^{x(a+1) + r} p) - 2^{x(a+1) + r + 1} p \leq 2^{x(a+1) + r + 1} - p - 1 \leq 0,
$$
so \( 2^{x(a+1) + r} p \in S \) for \( 0 < r \leq a \).

For \( x+1 \) and \( y = 0 \),
$$
f(2^{(x+1)(a+1)} p) = \sigma(2^{(x+1)(a+1)} p) - 2^{(x+1)(a+1) + 1} p = 2^{(x+1)(a+2)} - (p + 1) > 0,
$$
so \( 2^{(x+1)(a+1)} p \notin S \).

Thus, \( 2^r p \notin S \) if and only if \( a+1 \mid r \).

Now, consider \( n = 2^k p \) such that \( \sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d = n \). For \( n = 2^k p \) with \( k > a \),
$$
\sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d = \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} 2^i p = (2^k - 1)p - p = (2^k - 2)p < 2^k p,
$$
a contradiction. Therefore, \( k \leq a \).

For \( k = a \),
$$
\sum_{d \mid n, d < n, d \in S} d = \sum_{i=0}^{a} 2^i p = (2^{a+1} - 1)p - p = (2^{a+1} - 2)p = 2^a p = n,
$$
which holds.

Thus, the only solutions are \( n = 2^a p \) where \( a = \lfloor \log_2(p+1) \rfloor - 1 \).
  [24] For the inductive step, assume \( 2^{x(a+1) - y} p \in S \) for \( 0 \leq y \leq a \) and some \( x \geq 1 \). Then,
\[
f(2^{x(a+1) - y + 1} p) \leq \sigma(2^{x(a+1) - y + 1} p) - 2^{x(a+1) - y + 2} p = 2^{x(a+1) - y + 2} - (p + 1) \leq 0,
\]
so \( 2^{x(a+1) - y + 1} p \in S \). For \( 2^{x(a+1)} p \),
\[
f(2^{x(a+1)} p) = \sigma(2^{x(a+1)} p) - 2^{x(a+1) + 1} p = 2^{x(a+1) + 1} - (p + 1) > 0,
\]
which means \( 2^{x(a+1)} p \notin S \). This completes the induction.

Thus, \( 2^r p \notin S \) if and only if \( a+1 \mid r \). For \( n = 2^k p \) with \( k \) not a multiple of \( a+1 \), we have \( f(n) < 0 \), so \( n \in S \). For \( k \) a multiple of \( a+1 \), we need
\[
